# Line endings, and why this file is load-bearing.
#
# Sealed artifacts in this repository are hashed BYTE FOR BYTE. LOCK.tsv records a
# file_sha256 for every part, and verify.py recomputes it on read. If Git rewrites a
# single byte on the way out of the repository, that hash no longer matches and the
# library reports itself as tampered.
#
# Git does exactly that by default on Windows: `core.autocrlf=true` converts LF to CRLF
# on checkout. The bytes on disk then differ from the bytes that were sealed, and every
# part fails its file hash — on a clean clone, for a reader who has changed nothing.
#
# That is not hypothetical. It shipped: the first public clone of this repository failed
# 30 of 30 parts on a Windows machine, because the working tree it was sealed in had been
# assembled by copying files rather than by `git checkout`, so the conversion never
# happened for us. The sequences were fine throughout — seq_sha256 matched, since line
# endings do not change the bases — but the byte seal is the check that is supposed to
# notice a file changing underneath you, and it was being defeated by the version control
# system itself.
#
# So: never translate anything that is hashed.

# Source files normalise to LF, in the repository and in the working tree, on every OS.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Hashed artifacts are treated as binary. Byte-for-byte, no exceptions, no conversion.
*.gb      -text
*.gbk     -text
*.fasta   -text
*.fa      -text
*.tsv     -text
*.ttl     -text
LOCK.root -text
